Enable TCP keepalive on accepted serve connections

`probe-rs serve` releases a Session (and the debug probe it holds) when
the per-connection task ends, which happens when the websocket reader
future resolves. A client that dies abruptly - kill -9, a yanked cable,
a crash - never sends a FIN, so `reader.next()` blocks forever, the
`select!` in `handle_socket` never resolves, the task never returns, and
the Session stays pinned. The next client then fails to open the probe
with "interface is busy (errno 16)" and probe-rs exits 0 without
flashing, which surfaces as the wedge heuristic in probe-rs-runner.

Wrap the accept loop so every accepted connection gets SO_KEEPALIVE with
a 15s idle / 5s interval / 3 retry schedule. The kernel reaps the
half-open socket within ~30s, `reader.next()` returns None, and the
existing disconnect cleanup drops the Session and frees the probe.

socket2 is already in the dependency tree via tokio; this only adds a
direct, remote-feature-gated dependency on it.

Local fix only; no upstream issue or PR exists yet.

